# `M154d` / `C13`–`C22` — the known-answer plant for all six of tflw's locators, and for the three
# constructs that cannot be graded apart from them.
#
# `locator:button` (93 uses), `locator:text` (92), `locator:css` (69) and `locator:field` (65) are
# four of the most-used constructs in this repository, and before this file **not one of those uses
# could tell "resolved the right element" from "resolved an element"**. Every one of them names a
# thing that is unique on its page, so a `button` locator that had quietly degenerated into a text
# search would pass all ninety-three. `locator:list` and `locator:xpath` were at **zero** uses.
#
# That is what this file is for, and it is why it is a fixture page (`/locator-fixture`,
# `webV2/src/pages/LocatorFixturePage.tsx`) rather than a storefront flow. `D729` orders real flows
# first; a locator plant needs *deliberate* near-misses — a link wearing a button's text, a
# placeholder colliding with another field's label, four identical buttons where only the third is
# the answer — and a storefront that shipped those collisions would be a bug in the storefront. The
# precedent is `RenderFixturePage.tsx` (M45), which exists for the same shape of reason.
#
# ## How a wrong resolution is observed
#
# Every candidate on that page — the true target **and** every decoy — writes its own token into
# `#locator-readout` when interacted with. So a locator that lands on a decoy does not fail by
# not-found. It fails by reporting **the decoy's token**, which is the only way to tell the two
# apart. The wrong answers are named, not merely absent.
#
# ## What each locator's known answer actually is
#
#   `button`  resolves by ROLE. Three decoys carry the identical accessible text with a different
#             role (link, menuitem, bare div). Answer `button/true`; a text search reports one of
#             the others, or hard-errors on ambiguity.
#   `text`    resolves by rendered TEXT CONTENT. Four decoys carry the phrase in an attribute
#             instead — an input `value`, an `alt`, a `title`, an `aria-label`. Answer `text/true`.
#   `field`   resolves through `D6`'s closed three-step cascade, label → placeholder → role=textbox,
#             **in that fixed priority**. Two inputs answer to "Ship to", one by its `<label>` and
#             one by its placeholder. Nothing in this repository graded that order before, and an
#             order that flipped would still pass all sixty-five existing `fill field` uses.
#   `list`    resolves a role=list by its ACCESSIBLE NAME, and supplies the scope `within` narrows
#             to. Both lists hold a button named exactly "Remove", so an unscoped click on it is
#             ambiguous by construction — the pair below can only pass if `list` picked the named
#             list *and* `within` confined the search to it.
#   `css`     answers a positional question among four identical siblings. A resolution that took
#             the first match reports `css/1`.
#   `xpath`   the same shape, with an expression that deliberately does **not** start with `//`.
#             Playwright auto-detects a selector beginning with `//` or `..` as XPath, so an
#             implementation that forgot to prefix `xpath=` would still pass an xpath test written
#             the usual way. A leading `(` defeats that: the expression below resolves only because
#             `packages/runtime/src/browser.ts` prefixes it.
#
# ## The three constructs folded in, and why they are not separable
#
#   `step:click`       the readout only ever changes because a click was really dispatched. The
#                      first assertion below is the control: `none`, before anything is clicked.
#   `step:fill`        the field cascade's answer is *which input received the text*, which is a
#                      fact about the fill as much as about the locator.
#   `matcher:has-value` at **zero** uses in this repository before this file, and it is what reads
#                      the answer back out of both inputs.

@constructs @locators @ui
test "each locator resolves the element it names, and not the decoy wearing its clothes"
  open "/locator-fixture"

  # The control. Nothing has been clicked, so nothing has claimed the readout. Every assertion
  # after this one is only meaningful because this one holds.
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='none']" is visible

  # `button` — role, not text.
  click button "Archive shipment"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='button/true']" is visible

  # `text` — rendered content, not attributes.
  click text "Restock queued"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='text/true']" is visible

  # `css` — the third of four identical buttons, not the first.
  click css "[data-group-list='css'] li:nth-child(3) button"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='css/3']" is visible

  # `xpath` — the last of four, by an expression CSS cannot express, on a string that only
  # resolves if `xpath=` was really prefixed.
  click xpath "(//ol[@data-group-list='xpath']/li)[last()]//button"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='xpath/4']" is visible

  # `list` + `within` — the same button name in both lists, told apart only by the scope.
  within list "Backordered items"
    click button "Remove"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='list/items']" is visible

  within list "Backordered suppliers"
    click button "Remove"
  expect css "#locator-readout[data-token='list/suppliers']" is visible

  # `field` + `fill` + `has value` — the label wins the cascade, and the placeholder decoy is
  # still holding the value it started with. Both halves are the answer: "the right input was
  # filled" and "the wrong one was not".
  fill field "Ship to" with "GLASGOW"
  expect css "#field-by-label" has value "GLASGOW"
  expect css "#field-by-placeholder" has value "UNTOUCHED"

@constructs @locators @ui
test "without the scope, the same inner locator is ambiguous — this test MUST end red on its last line"
  # **This test is meant to fail**, and that red is `C19`'s second assertion.
  #
  # The twenty-five `within` uses that existed before this file all scope to a container whose inner
  # locator would have resolved uniquely on the whole page anyway. So a `within` that resolved its
  # scope and then searched the document would pass every one of them, and the block would read as
  # load-bearing while carrying nothing. Asserting that the scoped clicks *work* cannot see that —
  # it needs the unscoped case to be genuinely impossible.
  #
  # Here it is. Both lists hold a button named exactly "Remove", so at page scope the name matches
  # two elements and tflw hard-errors rather than picking one (`D7`: N>1 is always an error, and
  # `has count` is the only matcher exempt). If this line ever goes green, either the fixture lost a
  # decoy or `within` stopped being the thing that made the two tests above differ.
  open "/locator-fixture"
  click button "Remove"
